    "id": "10.21203/rs.3.rs-11177768/v1",
    "title": "HLA-B*57 as a Dominant Genetic Determinant of HIV-1 Elite Control: Comparative Reanalysis of MHC Variant Associations in European andAfrican-American Cohorts A reanalysis of published genomic association data",
    "abstract": "Abstract  Background  HIV-1 elite controllers (EC) maintain undetectable viral loads without antiretroviral therapy; a phenotype strongly linked to variation in the Major Histocompatibility Complex (MHC). HLA-B*57:01 is the most replicated genetic association with elite control in individuals of European ancestry, with HLA-B*57:03 identified as its counterpart in African-ancestry populations. Whether the structure of this association – its dose-dependence and independence from other MHC signals – has been characterized consistently across both ancestries has not been addressed in accessible form. Methods  Using published allele/carrier frequencies from Rahmouni et al. (2025, 2023), we analyzed a European cohort (543 elite controllers, 3,272 uninfected controls) and an African-American cohort (759 HIV-1-seropositive individuals enriched for elite controllers, cs1 + cs2, ICGH consortium). For the European cohort, we performed: (1) primary HLA-B*57 association testing, (2) a nine-variant MHC panel analysis, (3) a viral-load trend test, and (4) an HLA-B*57-Stratified Association Analysis. For the African-American cohort, we tested enrichment of rs1131446 (the HLA-B*57:03 tag SNP) relative to its background frequency in the general African population (1000 Genomes AFR, n = 661). All tests used Fisher's exact test with Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction where applicable. Results  In the European cohort, HLA-B*57 carriage was strongly associated with elite control (OR = 5.92, 95% CI: 4.66–7.51, p",
